WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.544
Hi, and welcome to Concord.

2
00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:05.516
It's a prototype from the ODI's Standards Hub for

3
00:00:05.516 --> 00:00:08.709
designing data standards together, in the open.

4
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:12.773
In about nine minutes I'll show you what Concord is for,

5
00:00:12.773 --> 00:00:16.616
how a standard grows from a conversation into a published release,

6
00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:19.449
and then the part I enjoy most: building an app

7
00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:22.076
against one of these standards with a coding agent.

8
00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:24.086
This is the hub.

9
00:00:24.086 --> 00:00:27.430
Each card is a standard that a community looks after.

10
00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:32.202
We'll follow the volunteering standard, which describes volunteering opportunities,

11
00:00:32.202 --> 00:00:36.280
the organisations that offer them, and the people who give their time.

12
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:40.531
Every standard here is an ontology, written in OWL,

13
00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:44.423
the web's language for describing concepts and how they relate.

14
00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:47.036
Concord has three goals.

15
00:00:47.036 --> 00:00:48.766
The first is rigour.

16
00:00:48.766 --> 00:00:53.039
Standards are strict OWL, a reasoner keeps them logically consistent,

17
00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:56.138
and Concord checks them against the FAIR principles:

18
00:00:56.138 --> 00:01:00.190
findable, accessible, interoperable and reusable.

19
00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.511
So data built on them is easier to find and combine.

20
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:06.799
The second is that the people who use a standard

21
00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.295
should shape it, whether they join a call,

22
00:01:09.295 --> 00:01:13.744
talk to an interviewer, or are a coding agent working on someone's app.

23
00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:18.261
And the third is governance without endless meetings.

24
00:01:18.261 --> 00:01:22.313
Every change is classified, checked and reviewed asynchronously,

25
00:01:22.313 --> 00:01:25.361
under rules the community agrees for itself.

26
00:01:26.162 --> 00:01:27.509
Let's look inside.

27
00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:30.736
The Browse page shows the classes and their relationships,

28
00:01:30.736 --> 00:01:34.704
so you can get a feel for the model before you read a line of code.

29
00:01:35.507 --> 00:01:39.420
Source and export shows the same ontology however you need it.

30
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.448
Turtle is the canonical form, and it's what we keep in git.

31
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.955
LinkML is there too, for teams that generate

32
00:01:46.955 --> 00:01:49.579
schemas and documentation from one model.

33
00:01:50.696 --> 00:01:54.167
And there are SHACL shapes, generated from the OWL.

34
00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:58.858
SHACL is the W3C's language for describing the shape data should have,

35
00:01:58.858 --> 00:02:02.294
so apps can validate their data against the standard.

36
00:02:03.101 --> 00:02:08.627
The shapes are also available in SHACL compact syntax, which is far easier to read.

37
00:02:08.627 --> 00:02:10.859
Each shape fits on a few lines.

38
00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.249
And for developers there's a Classes format:

39
00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:16.989
JavaScript and TypeScript classes generated

40
00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:21.610
from those shapes, which an app can import straight from the standard's address.

41
00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.206
We'll use them in a moment.

42
00:02:24.011 --> 00:02:28.365
On the Query page you can ask questions of the ontology in SPARQL.

43
00:02:28.365 --> 00:02:33.467
The query engine is compiled to WebAssembly and runs right here in your browser.

44
00:02:34.268 --> 00:02:36.114
If you'd rather not write SPARQL,

45
00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:39.272
describe what you want in the chat beside the editor.

46
00:02:39.272 --> 00:02:44.377
It writes the query for you to check and run, and each follow-up message refines it.

47
00:02:45.178 --> 00:02:47.593
Changes start with requirements.

48
00:02:47.593 --> 00:02:53.061
People describe how they'd use the standard as use cases, with competency questions:

49
00:02:53.061 --> 00:02:56.068
questions the ontology must be able to answer,

50
00:02:56.068 --> 00:02:59.414
like which opportunities suit someone free on weekends.

51
00:03:00.216 --> 00:03:02.735
Requirements also come from meetings.

52
00:03:02.735 --> 00:03:05.197
You can upload a Google Meet transcript.

53
00:03:05.197 --> 00:03:08.668
And with a meeting-bot provider configured, you can invite a bot that

54
00:03:08.668 --> 00:03:13.486
joins the call, takes notes, and asks clarifying questions in the chat.

55
00:03:14.288 --> 00:03:16.889
Requirements become change requests.

56
00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:19.605
The model never writes the ontology directly.

57
00:03:19.605 --> 00:03:24.397
It proposes terms, and Concord compiles them into validated edits.

58
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.626
Each change is classified as a patch,

59
00:03:27.626 --> 00:03:32.166
a minor change or a major one, using explicit versioning rules.

60
00:03:32.166 --> 00:03:36.752
Then it's validated: strict checks, the reasoner, a check for alignment

61
00:03:36.752 --> 00:03:41.735
with FAIR principles, and any competency tests that have been supplied.

62
00:03:42.538 --> 00:03:45.975
Finally it's reviewed under the standard's governance policy.

63
00:03:45.975 --> 00:03:48.018
That might mean a steward's sign-off,

64
00:03:48.018 --> 00:03:52.517
a review period where silence means consent, or a formal vote.

65
00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:55.629
The policy is the community's choice.

66
00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:01.005
Start from a template, like lazy consensus or a W3C-style working group,

67
00:04:01.005 --> 00:04:06.537
and tune it: who signs off, how long reviews run, and what's automated.

68
00:04:07.338 --> 00:04:09.927
Approved changes go into releases.

69
00:04:09.927 --> 00:04:12.354
Each release gets a proper semantic version,

70
00:04:12.354 --> 00:04:16.481
every format, and for major versions, a migration script.

71
00:04:17.282 --> 00:04:19.035
So that's the standards side.

72
00:04:19.035 --> 00:04:20.991
Now let's put on a developer's hat.

73
00:04:21.793 --> 00:04:24.278
This is Helping Hands, a small web app that

74
00:04:24.278 --> 00:04:27.076
lists volunteering opportunities near you.

75
00:04:27.076 --> 00:04:30.231
Its data is described with the volunteering standard.

76
00:04:31.031 --> 00:04:32.923
Here's the interesting bit.

77
00:04:32.923 --> 00:04:36.523
The app doesn't hand-write any code for the data model.

78
00:04:36.523 --> 00:04:40.749
It imports JavaScript classes straight from the standard's web address,

79
00:04:40.749 --> 00:04:45.085
and Concord generates those classes from the standard's SHACL shapes.

80
00:04:45.886 --> 00:04:48.881
To work on the app, I'll use Claude Code on the web,

81
00:04:48.881 --> 00:04:51.343
connected to Concord through MCP,

82
00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:56.788
the Model Context Protocol: an open standard for plugging tools into AI agents.

83
00:04:56.788 --> 00:05:00.881
Connecting it is one custom connector, with Concord's address.

84
00:05:01.684 --> 00:05:03.356
One note before we start.

85
00:05:03.356 --> 00:05:07.292
The Claude Code session you'll see is a scripted demonstration.

86
00:05:07.292 --> 00:05:10.577
Its calls to Concord are real, but the agent's replies

87
00:05:10.577 --> 00:05:13.465
and the code it writes were prepared in advance.

88
00:05:14.268 --> 00:05:16.578
The first time Claude Code uses it,

89
00:05:16.578 --> 00:05:20.177
a browser window opens so you can sign in to the hub.

90
00:05:20.177 --> 00:05:22.871
And the Connect page has one-click install links

91
00:05:22.871 --> 00:05:26.572
for other agents too, like Codex and Cursor.

92
00:05:27.377 --> 00:05:28.921
Here's the feature.

93
00:05:28.921 --> 00:05:31.359
Volunteers want to log the hours they've given,

94
00:05:31.359 --> 00:05:34.926
and organisers want to see the total for each opportunity.

95
00:05:35.728 --> 00:05:40.404
I'll ask Claude Code to build it, using the volunteering standard for the data.

96
00:05:42.073 --> 00:05:45.614
Before writing any code, Claude checks the standard.

97
00:05:45.614 --> 00:05:50.693
It searches for anything about hours, and finds hours per week on time commitment.

98
00:05:51.493 --> 00:05:55.498
But that's the time a role expects, not time someone actually gave.

99
00:05:55.498 --> 00:05:58.259
So the concept we need is missing from the standard.

100
00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:02.878
In the past, that's where you'd stop and wait for the standard to change.

101
00:06:02.878 --> 00:06:05.037
Instead, Claude creates a fork:

102
00:06:05.037 --> 00:06:08.802
my own development copy of the ontology, right on the hub.

103
00:06:09.603 --> 00:06:13.597
It adds the missing class to the fork, a volunteering activity,

104
00:06:13.597 --> 00:06:18.403
with the hours given, who gave them, which opportunity, and the date.

105
00:06:18.403 --> 00:06:21.306
The new terms keep the standard's own namespace,

106
00:06:21.306 --> 00:06:24.545
so my data stays valid when they land upstream.

107
00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:28.948
And here's the fork in the hub, next to the standard it came from,

108
00:06:28.948 --> 00:06:32.402
with its changes and a link back to the main ontology.

109
00:06:33.203 --> 00:06:37.951
Claude also proposes the change upstream, so the community can consider it.

110
00:06:37.951 --> 00:06:39.917
Nobody has to wait for anybody.

111
00:06:40.718 --> 00:06:42.320
The fork has its own address,

112
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.360
and it serves generated classes just like the main standard does.

113
00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.358
Here's the module, with a class for the new volunteering activity.

114
00:06:51.159 --> 00:06:56.209
So the only change Claude needs to make to use the new concept is the import line.

115
00:06:56.209 --> 00:06:57.873
It now points at my fork.

116
00:06:58.673 --> 00:07:02.237
Then it writes the feature itself: reading activities through the

117
00:07:02.237 --> 00:07:06.510
generated classes, and adding up the hours for each opportunity.

118
00:07:07.311 --> 00:07:11.618
Back in the app, each opportunity now shows the hours volunteers have given,

119
00:07:11.618 --> 00:07:14.181
and there's a running total for me at the top.

120
00:07:14.982 --> 00:07:17.594
Let's log a couple of hours at the food bank.

121
00:07:17.594 --> 00:07:20.180
And the totals update straight away.

122
00:07:20.981 --> 00:07:25.265
Meanwhile, my proposal is on the hub like any other change request.

123
00:07:25.265 --> 00:07:27.773
Concord has classified it as a minor change,

124
00:07:27.773 --> 00:07:31.456
because it only adds terms, and the validation has passed.

125
00:07:32.258 --> 00:07:35.892
Other app owners don't need to read every proposal either.

126
00:07:35.892 --> 00:07:40.513
They can connect their app's tests to Concord and switch on autopilot.

127
00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:48.763
Now, whenever a change is proposed, their tests run against it.

128
00:07:48.763 --> 00:07:52.467
If nothing breaks, Concord votes yes on their behalf.

129
00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:54.986
In this demo the test run is simulated:

130
00:07:54.986 --> 00:07:58.448
we report a passing result to Concord ourselves.

131
00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.456
A few days later, the change has been approved

132
00:08:01.456 --> 00:08:04.266
and released in a new version of the standard.

133
00:08:05.066 --> 00:08:08.537
At the start of my next session, Claude checks my forks,

134
00:08:08.537 --> 00:08:11.022
sees that the volunteering activity is now in the

135
00:08:11.022 --> 00:08:14.574
released standard, and offers to move me back.

136
00:08:15.376 --> 00:08:19.277
It's one line again: the import goes back to the main standard.

137
00:08:19.277 --> 00:08:23.552
The app works exactly as before, and my fork has done its job.

138
00:08:24.353 --> 00:08:25.967
That's Concord.

139
00:08:25.967 --> 00:08:28.208
Rigorous, open standards that grow from the

140
00:08:28.208 --> 00:08:30.611
people who use them, with governance that doesn't

141
00:08:30.611 --> 00:08:32.910
need a meeting, and room for developers to

142
00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.768
keep building while the standard catches up.

143
00:08:36.571 --> 00:08:39.961
It's an early prototype, and we'd love your feedback.

144
00:08:39.961 --> 00:08:43.337
Try it yourself at the link below, and thanks for watching.
